✅ Fibonacci Number Calculation – Theory, Steps, and Complexities

The Fibonacci sequence is:

F(0) = 0, F(1) = 1
F(n) = F(n–1) + F(n–2) for n ≥ 2

We can compute this using:

Recursive method (direct recursion)

Non-recursive method (iterative / dynamic)

Let’s break down both.

✅ 1. Recursive Approach (Naive Recursion)
✅ Concept

This method follows the mathematical definition directly.

To compute F(n), the algorithm calls:

F(n–1)

F(n–2)

Each of these calls further calls two more functions, and so on.

This forms a binary recursion tree.

✅ Step Count Intuition

Let T(n) = number of steps to compute F(n).

Because the algorithm calls itself twice for each non-base case:

T(n) = T(n–1) + T(n–2) + 1

This recurrence grows like the Fibonacci sequence itself.

Step count ≈ F(n)
which grows exponentially.

✅ Time Complexity

Because the recursion tree has exponential size:

✅ Time: O(2ⁿ) (more precisely ~O(1.618ⁿ))
This is extremely slow for large n.

✅ Space Complexity

Space = height of recursion tree = depth of function calls

Depth = n

✅ Space: O(n)
This is from the call stack.

✅ 2. Non-Recursive Approach (Iterative / Dynamic Programming)
✅ Concept

Iteratively compute Fibonacci values from bottom up:

Start with F(0) and F(1)

Keep adding the two previous numbers to get the next one

Repeat until you reach F(n)

This avoids repeated recomputation.

✅ Step Count Intuition

There is one loop running from 2 to n, and each iteration does constant work:

Step count ≈ (n – 1)

So step count grows linearly.

✅ Time Complexity

✅ Time: O(n)
Much faster than recursion.

✅ Space Complexity

Two common possibilities:

A. Using just 2 variables (most common)

Keep only prev and curr.

✅ Space: O(1)

B. Using an array to store all values

Useful if you need every Fibonacci value, not just the final one.

✅ Space: O(n)

✅ Comparing Both Approaches
Approach	Time Complexity	Space Complexity	Step Count	Notes
Recursive (naive)	O(2ⁿ)	O(n)	exponential (≈F(n))	Slow, repeated calls
Iterative	O(n)	O(1)	linear	Fast and efficient
✅ Short, Exam-Ready Summary

Use this when writing your practical viva/theory answer:

Recursive Fibonacci uses the direct mathematical recurrence. It recomputes values multiple times, forming an exponential recursion tree.

Time: O(2ⁿ)

Space: O(n) due to recursion depth

Step count grows like Fibonacci numbers → exponential

Iterative Fibonacci computes values from bottom-up using a loop, with no repeated work.

Time: O(n)

Space: O(1) (two variables)

Step count grows linearly
